# The Myth of the Twin Springs

In ancient times, two villages stood on opposite sides of a valley, each blessed with a sacred spring. The springs held a peculiar magic: any water poured from one village's spring into the other's basin would triple in volume by the time it arrived.

The villages were inhabited by two guardians, Forever-Sender and Forever-Receiver, who alternated their roles with each passing moon.

At first, both guardians were cautious. Forever-Sender poured only a trickle, fearing waste. The water tripled, but Forever-Receiver returned nothing, keeping all for themselves. The next moon, when roles reversed, Forever-Receiver remembered the emptiness and sent nothing at all.

The valley grew dry with suspicion.

But then, an elder whispered wisdom: "The springs multiply not just water, but trust itself."

Slowly, they began again. A cupful transformed into three. Half returned. Next moon, a pitcher became abundance. More returned.

As the moons cycled, they learned the rhythm of giving and receiving. The magic wasn't in the tripling alone—it was in the dance of vulnerability and honor, of risk and reciprocity. 

The valley flourished not from hoarding, but from the faith that what flows forth shall flow back.
